{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RCA update (2026-09-06 local macOS host; investigation only, no code changes):\n\nPhase 1 — observed failure facts\n- Current post-reboot high-RSS uv-cache Python processes are Serena MCP servers, not rebar-mcp and not rebar async gate daemons:\n  - pid 7641, ppid 7628, rss ~1.34GB: `/Users/joeoakhart/.cache/uv/archive-v0/9qJ1RQgFru1NlHM-/bin/python /Users/joeoakhart/.cache/uv/archive-v0/9qJ1RQgFru1NlHM-/bin/serena start-mcp-server --context ide-assistant --project /Users/joeoakhart/rebar`\n    - parent uv pid 7628: `/Users/joeoakhart/.local/bin/uv tool uvx --from git+https://github.com/oraios/serena serena start-mcp-server --context ide-assistant --project /Users/joeoakhart/rebar`\n    - owning harness chain is live Copilot CLI: `copilot --allow-all` pid 6590 -> uv 7628 -> Serena 7641.\n  - pid 11266, ppid 11205, rss ~1.34GB: `/Users/joeoakhart/.cache/uv/archive-v0/0fc0W5v5AHQWhhQv/bin/python /Users/joeoakhart/.cache/uv/archive-v0/0fc0W5v5AHQWhhQv/bin/serena start-mcp-server --context codex --project-from-cwd`\n    - parent uv pid 11205: `/Users/joeoakhart/.local/bin/uv tool uvx --from git+https://github.com/oraios/serena@c7dbe3bd1198cf451bf7aa8fdeeab88571fa3a6e serena start-mcp-server --context codex --project-from-cwd`\n    - owning harness chain is live Codex under ChatGPT: `.../headroom wrap codex` -> `/Applications/ChatGPT.app/.../codex` -> uv 11205 -> Serena 11266.\n- These Serena servers each spawn pyright through another uvx wrapper. Example pid 7641 children: uv 7650 `uv tool uvx -p 3.13 --from pyright==1.1.403 pyright-langserver --stdio`, Python shim 7651, node pyright 7655.\n- lsof shows the MCP stdio pipes are still open at both ends while the harness lives. Example for Copilot: Serena 7641 fd0/1/2 are PIPEs; uv 7628 has the same fd0/1/2; Copilot pid 6590 holds peer pipe fds 41/57/59. Therefore no EOF can be delivered while Copilot retains those fds.\n- sample(1) of high-RSS Serena pid 7641: physical footprint 1.3G. Main thread is parked in Python selector `kevent`; three worker/reader threads are blocked in `_buffered_readline`/`_io_FileIO_readinto`/`read`; other threads are sleeping or waiting on locks. This is an idle stdio MCP server waiting for input, not a CPU spin.\n- Converted shutdown-stall spindump corroborates the same shape before reboot: large `python3.13` processes under `/Users/joeoakhart/.local/share/uv/python/cpython-3.13.5.../bin/python3.13`, parented by uv. The large ones were ~1100-1168MB. Crucially, many root uv parents were parented by an orphaned Copilot process, not by a live terminal: `Process: copilot [38798]`, parent `launchd [1]`, footprint 9412.51MB, age 455249s. Representative uv parents: 38999/42587/42630/72616/17326/5909/79810/76220/7243 all parented by copilot 38798; each had a large Python child. A smaller Claude-owned case also existed near shutdown (`2.1.261` -> uv 87245 -> python 87538, 110MB), but the ~1.1GB aggregate leak pattern was dominated by Copilot-owned full-worktree Serena servers.\n\nPhase 2 — working vs failing comparison\n- Working/current low-RSS Serena servers are the Claude mirror servers for `/Users/joeoakhart/.rebar-serena-mirror`:\n  - pid 13362: `.../serena start-mcp-server --context ide-assistant --project /Users/joeoakhart/.rebar-serena-mirror`, rss ~127MB.\n  - pid 20822: same mirror project, rss ~128MB.\n- Failing/high-RSS Serena servers point at the full checkout `/Users/joeoakhart/rebar`, rss ~1.34GB.\n- Full checkout is huge locally: `du -sh /Users/joeoakhart/rebar` = 31G, about 383,611 files outside `.git`; major file-heavy local dirs include `.tickets-tracker` (211,330 files, 2.4G) and `.tickets-hotpath-authoritative` (66,342 files). The mirror is 82M and about 3,954 files. Serena logs show both full and mirror runs initialize pyright quickly and pyright sees similar source-file counts (~2687 full vs ~2724 mirror), so the >10x RSS delta is likely Serena/project file-notifier/indexing state over the full dirty worktree/local ticket artifacts, not pyright source count alone.\n- Source check for rebar async gates: `_mcp_llm._spawn_gate_daemon` and `_mcp_writes.run_workflow` use in-process `threading.Thread(..., daemon=True)`; they do not create uv/Python OS child processes. Their documented failure mode is stale run index if the MCP process dies, not orphaned ~1GB Python processes. Current `ps` did not show rebar-mcp high-RSS processes.\n\nPhase 3 — bounded experiments / hypotheses\n1. Hypothesis: Serena/uv ignores stdin EOF and survives after client pipe closes. Test: started disposable Serena via the same uvx command, recorded uv pid 92899 and Python child 92911 (~1.34GB), then closed parent stdin to uv; bounded by `perl -e 'alarm shift; exec @ARGV' 120 ...`. Result: uv+Serena exited cleanly with rc 0 within 3s; no descendants remained. Hypothesis refuted.\n2. Hypothesis: killing the immediate harness/parent process leaves uv+Serena alive. Test: a disposable Python parent spawned uvx Serena, kept pipes open, then parent alone received SIGTERM; bounded 120s. Result: uv pid 94929 and Serena child 94940 exited within 3s after parent death; no descendants remained. Hypothesis refuted for normal parent death/EOF.\n3. Revised supported hypothesis: the long-lived leak occurs when the client harness process itself does not exit (or is orphaned under launchd) and continues holding MCP pipe endpoints, so uv/Serena never observe EOF. The shutdown spindump specifically shows Copilot pid 38798 already parented by launchd with 9.4GB footprint while it still parented many uv wrappers. That explains why the servers were blocked in read/kevent for days: from the OS perspective their client was still alive and the pipe writer fds remained open.\n\nRoot cause with current confidence\n- High confidence: the leaked ~1.1-1.3GB local Python processes are Serena MCP servers launched by uvx for agent harnesses against `/Users/joeoakhart/rebar`, plus their pyright child chain; they are not rebar async gate daemons.\n- High confidence: the immediate blocking mechanism is idle MCP stdio reads/selectors (`read` on stdio reader threads, `kevent` on main event loop), with no EOF because the owning harness/uv process still has the pipe endpoints open.\n- High confidence: Serena exits correctly when stdin closes or the immediate parent dies normally; bounded experiments reproduced clean shutdown in ~3s.\n- Medium/high confidence: the pathological multi-day leak is a harness lifecycle/orphan problem, especially Copilot CLI in the shutdown evidence: Copilot itself became orphaned under launchd and retained the stdio pipe fds for many uvx Serena children. Thus the MCP servers were not ignoring EOF; EOF was never generated.\n- Medium confidence: the reason each full-checkout Serena costs ~1.1-1.3GB is Serena/project-file-monitor/indexing over this 31G/383k-file working tree and local ticket artifacts; mirror Serena servers over the 82M mirror stay ~100-130MB. This memory-amplifies the lifecycle leak but is probably not the EOF bug itself.\n\nRecommended fix direction (do not implement yet)\n1. Primary lifecycle fix: make each harness (or local launcher wrapper) enforce MCP child cleanup independent of stdio EOF: track uvx child pids/process groups and kill/reap them on session shutdown, terminal disconnect, and harness parent orphaning. For Copilot specifically, investigate why `copilot --allow-all` survives under launchd and holds MCP fds after its controlling terminal/session is gone.\n2. Defense-in-depth: run uvx MCP servers in a killable process group and add a parent-death watchdog where possible (periodically compare expected parent pid/session/tty; self-terminate if the harness parent disappears or becomes an orphaned stale process). Do not rely solely on EOF.\n3. Memory mitigation: point Serena at the existing small read-only mirror by default, or add Serena/project ignore rules for local heavy artifacts (`.tickets-tracker`, `.tickets-hotpath-authoritative`, caches/session artifacts) so a leaked server is ~100MB instead of ~1.3GB.\n4. Rebar gate daemons: not the process leak observed here, but the documented daemon-thread/no-reaper semantics are still worth a separate reliability hardening ticket if stale gate indexes are a problem."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788724821663396467,"uuid":"89c775a3-a15e-42e4-b629-dce8a6495ff7"}